/** @page python_qore_object_lifecycle_managementguide Managing the Lifecycle of Qore objects from Python

    @tableofcontents

    %Python objects for %Qore objects hold strong references to the %Qore objects: a %Qore object exists as long as
    %Qore code or %Python code references it, and its destructor runs when the last reference is released, as with
    %Qore's own deterministic garbage collection.  %Python also releases its objects deterministically, when their
    last reference is released, so a %Qore object that %Python code creates, receives or returns is destroyed when
    neither %Qore nor %Python references it anymore.

    @section python_qore_object_lifecycle_cycles Reference Cycles

    %Python objects that reference each other in a cycle are released by %Python's cycle collector, which runs
    automatically from time to time (and when \c gc.collect() is called) instead of when the last reference is
    released.  An instance of a %Python class that inherits a %Qore class is part of such a cycle (the %Python object
    holds its %Qore object, whose data holds the %Python object): while %Qore code references the %Qore object, the
    %Python object exists as well, and when only the %Python object references it, the cycle collector releases both,
    and the %Qore object's destructor runs at that time.

    Cycles that pass through %Qore references are not seen by %Python's cycle collector, as %Python cannot see the
    references that %Qore objects hold; such cycles are released when the %Python program
    (@ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram") that holds the %Python objects is deleted, or when the cycle is broken
    by removing one of the references (ex: by assigning \c None to an attribute):
    - an instance of a %Python class that inherits a %Qore class whose %Qore object references the instance (ex:
      \c self.me \c = \c self, which is the %Qore member \c me), directly or through other %Qore objects
    - a %Python object referenced by a %Qore object (ex: a member, a list or a hash) that references the %Qore object
      (ex: \c p.a \c = \c a and \c a.m \c = \c p)

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
class Node(QoreBase):   # QoreBase is a Qore class
    pass

n = Node()
n.me = n        # a cycle through the Qore member me
n.me = None     # breaks the cycle: n is released when its last Python reference is released
    @endcode

    Cycles of %Qore objects alone are released by %Qore, and cycles of %Python objects alone by %Python.

    The destructors of %Qore objects released by %Python run without the %Python GIL, on the thread that releases
    them.

    @section python_qore_object_lifecycle_explicit Explicit Qore Object Lifecycle Management

    The lifecycle of %Qore objects can additionally be managed explicitly by using the
    @ref Python::PythonProgram::setSaveObjectCallback() "PythonProgram::setSaveObjectCallback()" method to set a
    callback that is called every time a %Qore object is created using the %Python API; the callback runs without the
    %Python GIL.  A reference that the callback keeps keeps the object alive.

    This callback must take a single object argument as in the following example:

    @code{.py}
hash<string, object> object_cache;
code callback = sub (object obj) {
    # save object in object cache, so it doesn't go out of scope
    object_cache{obj.uniqueHash()} = obj;
}
PythonProgram::setSaveObjectCallback(callback);
    @endcode
*/
